--- Interpretation: Which Thresholds are Weak vs Strong Evidence ---
1. WEAK EVIDENCE (High Base Rate by Chance):
   - Rain >= 2.5 mm/24h fires 52.8% of the time during monsoon. Anyone reporting 'rain' during July-August has >50% chance of matching purely at random.
   - Rain >= 15.6 mm/24h fires 27.8% during monsoon. Moderate rain is common across coastal and central India in peak monsoon.
   - Heatwave >= 40.0 C fires 41.7% in May across North, Central, and Western plains by default.
   - Conclusion: Low thresholds (light rain, moderate heat) provide WEAK corroboration in season. Corroboration weights must be dampened for seasonal baseline events.
2. STRONG EVIDENCE (Low Base Rate / Specific Event):
   - Rain >= 64.5 mm/24h (IMD Heavy Rain) fires only ~5.6% during monsoon and 0.0% in non-monsoon. A report matching >= 64.5 mm is strong corroboration.
   - Dense Fog (Visibility < 200 m) fires only 0.0% in monsoon/summer and only selectively in winter (North India). It is highly diagnostic.
   - Wind Gusts >= 62.0 km/h (Gale) fires < 3% overall, making it a strong indicator of cyclonic storms or squalls.
